      "text": "To find the maximum value of the expression \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we start by expanding the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can split this fraction into three separate fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nLet's denote \\(A = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), \\(B = \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\), and \\(C = \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\). Therefore, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\nA + B + C.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the maximum value of \\(A + B + C\\). To do this, we use the fact that for any positive real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), the expression \\(\\frac{x^2 + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\) is always equal to 1. This is because:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1.\n\\]\n\nHowever, this is a special case where the terms \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) sum to a specific value. To find this value, we use the identity for the sum of squares of the terms:\n\n\\[\nA + B + C = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + y^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the numerator \\(x^2 + 2xy + y^2\\) as \\((x + y)^2\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nTo find the maximum value, we consider the case when \\(x = y\\). If \\(x = y\\), then the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + x)^2}{x^2 + x^2} = \\frac{(2x)^2}{2x^2} = \\frac{4x^2}{2x^2} = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
