      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we start by expanding the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we can separate the fraction into three separate fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe will focus on each term individually. Consider the first term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite this term as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 - \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nNow, let's analyze the second term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the following form:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + 2xy + y^2) \\left( \\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{2}{y^2} + \\frac{1}{y^2} \\right) \\geq (1 + \\sqrt{2} + 1)^2 = (2 + \\sqrt{2})^2 = 6 + 4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + 2xy + y^2) \\left( \\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{2}{y^2} + \\frac{1}{y^2} \\right) \\geq 6 + 4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} \\geq \\frac{6 + 4\\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{6 + 4\\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}}\\), we rationalize the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6 + 4\\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{(6 + 4\\sqrt{2})(2 - \\sqrt{2})}{(2 + \\sqrt{2})(2 - \\sqrt{2})} = \\frac{12 - 6\\sqrt{2} + 8\\sqrt{2} - 8}{4 - 2} = \\frac{4 + 2\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 2 + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} \\geq 2 + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow, let's consider the third term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2} \\leq 1 + 1 = 2.\n\\]\n\nCombining all these results, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} \\geq 1 + (2 + \\sqrt{2}) = 3 + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nEquality holds when \\(\\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{1}{2 + \\sqrt{2}}\\). This occurs when \\(x = y\\). Therefore, the maximum value is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3 + \\sqrt{2}}.\n\\]",
